# Symbulator circuit book

title: Circuits for Lesson 8 of the Tutorial

[AS7's Example 11.5 (maximum average power)]

e,1,0,10
r1,1,2,4
r2,2,0,8-6j
r3,2,3,5j

analysis: ac
note: Find the load impedance that maximises the average power drawn from the circuit, and that maximum power. The load that draws the most power is the conjugate of zeq. Version 9 has no separate apmax: in AC, pmax is already the average maximum power.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/circuit/as7e1105.png
omega: omega
tool: th
n1: 3
n2: 0
rounding: 5
si: no
units: yes
rms: no
polar: no


[AS7's Example 11.10 (power factor)]

e,1,0,30
r1,1,2,6
r2,2,0,-2j
r3,2,0,4

analysis: ac
note: Find the power factor of the circuit as seen by the source, and the average power the source delivers — the Evaluate line, -pe: 125.4 W. The values are RMS, so the RMS setting is on. The power factor comes from the pf mini-tool, given ve and -ie: 0.97342 leading. The minus sign matters -- ie is the current into the source, and without it the tool says lagging.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/circuit/as7e1110.png
omega: omega
rounding: 5
si: no
units: yes
rms: yes
polar: no
evaluate: -pe


[AS7's Example 11.13 (complex power)]

e,1,0,220
r1,1,2,4+2j
r2,2,0,15-10j

analysis: ac
note: Find the real and reactive power delivered by the source (the Evaluate line, -se) and absorbed by the transmission line (sr1) and the load (sr2). RMS values throughout.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/circuit/as7e1113.png
omega: omega
rounding: 5
si: no
units: yes
rms: yes
polar: no
evaluate: -se


