[AS7's Problem 11.75 (a and b, the power system)]

e,1,0,240
r1,1,0,80-50j
r2,1,0,120+70j
r3,1,0,60

analysis: ac
note: Parts (a) and (b): find the total complex power delivered (the Evaluate line, -se) and the power factor. pf with ve and -ie reads 0.99805 leading, which is what makes part (c) come out the way it does.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/circuit/as7p1175.png
omega: omega
rounding: 5
si: no
units: yes
rms: yes
polar: no
evaluate: -se


[AS7's Problem 11.75 (c, trying a capacitor)]

e,1,0,240
r1,1,0,80-50j
r2,1,0,120+70j
r3,1,0,60
c,1,0,x

analysis: ac
note: Part (c): solve im(se) = 0 for the parallel capacitance x, at 50 Hz. The answer comes back negative. No positive capacitance can work: part (b) showed the load is already leading, so a capacitor pushes it further the wrong way. Probably why the textbook gives no number here.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/circuit/as7p1175.png
omega: 2*pi*50
rounding: 5
si: no
units: yes
rms: yes
polar: no
solve_equations: im(se) = 0
solve_unknowns: x
solve_real_only: yes


[AS7's Problem 11.75 (c, an inductor instead)]

e,1,0,240
r1,1,0,80-50j
r2,1,0,120+70j
r3,1,0,60
l,1,0,x

analysis: ac
note: The same solve with an inductor in place of the capacitor: x = 1.5987 H. Positive, and putting it back in as the inductor's value brings the power factor to 1.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/circuit/as7p1175.png
omega: 2*pi*50
rounding: 5
si: no
units: yes
rms: yes
polar: no
solve_equations: im(se) = 0
solve_unknowns: x
solve_real_only: yes
